<DocsShell
  title="API reference"
  description="The public Python contracts and concrete components exposed by trivium."
  current="api"
>
  <span class="docs-eyebrow">API reference</span>
  <h1 class="docs-title">Primitives you can build on.</h1>
  <p class="docs-lede">The benchmark is the reference workload. The library is the useful part: small components with direct names and explicit boundaries.</p>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>Retrievers.</h2>
    <p>All retrievers expose the same search shape and return <code>list[SearchResult]</code>. Choose the implementation that matches your memory and latency budget.</p>
    <pre class="docs-code"><code>from trivium.retrieval.bm25 import Bm25
from trivium.retrieval.faiss import Faiss

bm25 = Bm25(k1=0.9, b=0.4)
bm25.add_documents(documents)
bm25_hits = bm25.search(query_texts, k=100)

flat = Faiss.Flat()
flat.add_documents(documents, vectors=document_vectors)
vector_hits = flat.search(query_vectors, k=100)</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>Fusion and reranking.</h2>
    <pre class="docs-code"><code>from trivium.fusion.rrf import Rrf
from trivium.reranking.registry import get_reranker

fused = Rrf(k=60).fuse([bm25_hits, vector_hits], top_k=50)
reranker = get_reranker("bge-large")
final_hits = reranker.rerank(query_text, candidates, top_k=10)</code></pre>
    <p>Registries are the extension point for new embedders, rerankers, fusion strategies, and pipeline modes. Add a concrete implementation to the relevant contract; keep the public surface direct.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>Command-line entry points.</h2>
    <div class="docs-table-wrap">
      <table class="docs-table">
